                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 917,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 656,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Transposes a 2-D tensor: rows become columns.\n\nThe short form of t.transpose 0 1, for the common case. A 7 by 6 tensor becomes 6 by 7; row 0 becomes column 0, so a picture is flipped across its diagonal.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.t\nNo arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\nA 1-D tensor (or a single number) comes back unchanged - a row has no columns to swap with. A tensor with 3 or more axes is an error: the console prints 't() expects a tensor with <= 2 dimensions' and nothing is sent. Use t.transpose or t.adjoint for those.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe transposed tensor. Its memory is not moved, so it is not contiguous: t.view refuses it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) turned through t.t lies on its side, 6 by 7, its top bar now down the left. t.info shows the shapes swap. Put the result through a second t.t and it would stand upright again - transposing twice is no change.\n\nRELATED:\nt.transpose swaps any two axes. t.adjoint swaps the last two axes of a tensor of any size. t.permute reorders all the axes. np.transpose is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 640,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Transposes a 2-D tensor: rows become columns.\n\nThe short form of t.transpose 0 1, for the common case. A 7 by 6 tensor becomes 6 by 7; row 0 becomes column 0, so a picture is flipped across its diagonal.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.t\nNo arguments.\n\nINPUTS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\nA 1-D tensor (or a single number) comes back unchanged - a row has no columns to swap with. A tensor with 3 or more axes is an error: the console prints 't() expects a tensor with <= 2 dimensions' and nothing is sent. Use t.transpose or t.adjoint for those.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe transposed tensor. Its memory is not moved, so it is not contiguous: t.view refuses it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) turned through t.t lies on its side, 6 by 7, its top bar now down the left. t.info shows the shapes swap. Put the result through a second t.t and it would stand upright again - transposing twice is no change.\n\nRELATED:\nt.transpose swaps any two axes. t.adjoint swaps the last two axes of a tensor of any size. t.permute reorders all the axes. np.transpose is the numpy version."
                },
